# Add flexible date formatting with language, weekday, Geez numerals, and time options to Ethiopian calendar

## Description

This update enhances the Ethiopian calendar library's date formatting capabilities by introducing a unified, flexible `format(options)` method on the `Kenat` class. Users can now customize output to include:

- Month and weekday names in multiple languages (`amharic`, `english`, etc.)
- Optionally show weekday names
- Optionally format day and year using Geez numerals
- Optionally include Ethiopian time with localized period suffixes

Additionally, standalone format helper functions (`formatStandard`, `formatInGeezAmharic`, `formatWithWeekday`, `formatWithTime`, etc.) were refactored to be private/internal helpers, accessible internally through class methods, improving encapsulation and consistency.

Comprehensive tests demonstrate flexible usage patterns, including combined options for weekday and time with language and numeral format control.

This update makes date formatting highly customizable, easier to use, and consistent across the library.

## Changelog

- Added `format(options)` method to `Kenat` class supporting:
  - `lang` option for month and weekday language
  - `showWeekday` option to include weekday name
  - `useGeez` option for Geez numeral formatting of day and year
  - `includeTime` option to append Ethiopian time string
- Refactored existing format helper functions as internal/private functions
- Updated `toString()`, `toISOString()`, and other class methods to use new formatting internally
- Added comprehensive test cases for all new formatting options
- Improved documentation and usage examples for flexible date formatting

## Usage Examples

```js
import Kenat from 'kenat';

const today = new Kenat(2016, 1, 10, 8, 30, 'day');

// Default: Amharic, no weekday, Arabic numerals, no time
console.log(today.format());
// "መስከረም 10 2016"

// English month name
console.log(today.format({ lang: 'english' }));
// "Meskerem 10 2016"

// Include weekday name in Amharic
console.log(today.format({ showWeekday: true }));
// "ማክሰኞ, መስከረም 10 2016"

// Include weekday + Geez numerals in Amharic
console.log(today.format({ showWeekday: true, useGeez: true }));
// "ማክሰኞ, መስከረም ፲ ፳፻፲፮"

// Include time, Amharic
console.log(today.format({ includeTime: true }));
// "መስከረም 10 2016 08:30 ጠዋት"

// Include weekday and time, English
console.log(today.format({ showWeekday: true, includeTime: true, lang: 'english' }));
// "Tuesday, Meskerem 10 2016 08:30 day"

# Migrate from Babel 6 to Babel 7 and Modernize Build Toolchain

## Overview

This PR migrates our codebase from Babel 6 to Babel 7, revamps the build and lint toolchain, and enables a suite of modern JavaScript syntax features. All changes are focused on development tooling and code/test syntax—**no intended functional changes**.

## Key Changes

### Dependencies
- **Removed:** Legacy `babel-cli`, `babel-core@6`, and associated presets/plugins
- **Added:** 
  - `@babel/core`, `@babel/preset-env`, `@babel/preset-react`
  - `@babel/plugin-proposal-*` plugins for modern syntax:
    - Optional chaining
    - Nullish coalescing
    - Private methods/fields
    - Numeric separators
    - Logical assignment
  - `@babel/eslint-parser`
- **Updated:** ESLint → 8.x, eslint-config-airbnb → 19.x

### Babel Configuration (`.babelrc`)
- Switch to `@babel/preset-env` and `@babel/preset-react`
- Enable new language features:
  - Private fields
  - Dynamic import syntax
  - BigInt support
- Simplify and unify loader options under `.babelrc`

### ESLint Configuration (`.eslintrc`, `.eslintignore`)
- Use `@babel/eslint-parser` for new syntax support
- Relax/override certain Airbnb rules for modern code patterns
- Ignore legacy test files until syntax support stabilizes

### Build Configuration
- **Webpack & sourcemaps configs:** Remove inline Babel/ESLint loader options from `webpack.config.js` and `sourcemaps.config.js`
- Rely on root configuration files for consistency

### Code & Test Updates
- Update React components (e.g., `Card.jsx`, `Container.jsx`) and Jest specs
- Modernize syntax to take advantage of new language features
- Align with new parser capabilities

## Scope

This migration affects:
- Build toolchain (Babel, Webpack, sourcemaps)
- Linting

# Modern JavaScript Features Enabled by Babel 7

The following document outlines the key modern JavaScript syntax features now available in our codebase thanks to the Babel 7 migration.

## 1. Nullish Coalescing (`??`)

**Problem:** Using `||` tramples any *falsy* author value, even when it was intentional.

**CaaS use-case:** An author might explicitly set:
- `resultsPerPage: 0` (→ "only featured cards, no paging")  
- `title: ''` (→ "hide the heading altogether")

**Before:**
```javascript
let pageSize = config.collection.resultsPerPage || 10;
// if author wrote 0, pageSize becomes 10 anyway!
```

**After:**
```javascript
let pageSize = config.collection.resultsPerPage ?? 10;
// only defaults on null or undefined – preserves 0 and ''
```

## 2. Logical Assignment (`||=`, `&&=`, `??=`)

**Problem:** Repetitive boilerplate when you merge defaults and toggle flags.

**CaaS use-case:** Selectively default or gate feature flags in `config.collection`.

**Before:**
```javascript
if (!cfg.showTotalResults) {
  cfg.showTotalResults = true;
}
if (cfg.partialLoadWithBackgroundFetch.enabled && !isMobile) {
  cfg.partialLoadWithBackgroundFetch.enabled = false;
}
```

**After:**
```javascript
cfg.showTotalResults ||= true;
cfg.partialLoadWithBackgroundFetch.enabled &&= !isMobile;
```

## 3. Optional Chaining (`?.`)

**Problem:** Nested `&&` guards litter your code and hide intent.

**CaaS use-case:** Picking apart deeply nested overlays or analytics data.

**Before:**
```javascript
const bannerText =
  props.overlays &&
  props.overlays.banner &&
  props.overlays.banner.description;
```

**After:**
```javascript
const bannerText = props.overlays?.banner?.description;
```

## 4. Optional Catch Binding (`catch {…}`)

**Problem:** You often have a `catch (e)` you never use, just to satisfy syntax.

**Why drop the `e`?**
- Cleaner, less noise
- No accidental lint warnings about unused variables

**Before:**
```javascript
try {
  await fetchData();
} catch (err) {
  // we don't care about the error object here
}
```

**After:**
```javascript
try {
  await fetchData();
} catch {
  // silent failure, or set some generic state
}
```

## 5. Numeric Separators (`1_000_000`)

**Problem:** Large literals (timeouts, batch sizes) become hard to parse at a glance.

**Use-case in CaaS:** Default debounce delays, analytics bulk-upload thresholds.

**Before:**
```javascript
const FETCH_TIMEOUT_MS = 300000;     // what is "300,000"? hard to scan
const BATCH_SIZE        = 1000000;   // one million?
```

**After:**
```javascript
const FETCH_TIMEOUT_MS = 300_000;    // immediately legible
const BATCH_SIZE       = 1_000_000;
```

## 6. BigInt (`12345678901234567890n`)

**Problem:** JS `Number` tops out safely at 2^53–1. Beyond that you get silent precision loss.

**CaaS use-case:**
- **Filter-bitmasking:** Pack up to 60 distinct filters into a single integer to speed up cache keys or URL tokens
- **Stable hashing:** Generate deterministic "signature" of a payload by multiplying and adding large primes without overflow

**Before (loses precision):**
```javascript
// pack first 60 filter flags into a JS Number
let mask = 0;
for (let i = 0; i < flags.length; i++) {
  if (flags[i]) mask |= (1 << i);
}
// if i > 31, you're already corrupting bits!
```

**After (full 64-bit safe):**
```javascript
let mask = 0n;
for (let i = 0n; i < BigInt(flags.length); i++) {
  if (flags[Number(i)]) mask |= (1n << i);
}
// you now have a true 64-bit+ mask, serialize as mask.toString()
```

## 7. Class Properties & Private Fields (`=`, `#`)

**Problem:** Manual `this.method = this.method.bind(this)` and unintentional public state.

**CaaS use-case:** In `CardFilterer` or `ConsonantPageModel` you track internal caches (e.g. last-seen sort key, reservoir sample).

**Before:**
```javascript
export default class CardFilterer {
  constructor(cards) {
    this.cards = cards;
    this.handleResize = this.handleResize.bind(this);
    this._cache = {};                // "private" by naming convention only
  }
  handleResize() { … }
  filter() { … }
}
```

**After:**
```javascript
export default class CardFilterer {
  #cache = {};                      // truly private
  cards;
  handleResize = () => { … };       // auto-bound
  
  constructor(cards) {
    this.cards = cards;
  }
  
  filter() { … }
}
```

## 8. Object Rest/Spread (`{…a, …b}`)

**Problem:** `Object.assign({}, a, b, {c: 3})` is verbose and easy to typo.

**CaaS use-case:** Merging default UI props with author overrides and runtime flags.

**Before:**
```javascript
const merged = Object.assign(
  {},
  defaultConfig.collection,
  userConfig.collection,
  { lazyLoad: isMobile }
);
```

**After:**
```javascript
const merged = {
  ...defaultConfig.collection,
  ...userConfig.collection,
  lazyLoad: isMobile,
};
```

## 9. Dynamic `import()` & Code-Splitting

**Problem:** Pulling in the entire Container/Helpers into your main bundle slows first-paint.

**CaaS use-case:** Only load the heavy `CardFilterer` or analytics code when the user actually opens the filter panel or scrolls to the bottom.

**Before:**
```javascript
// always in main.js
import CardFilterer from '../Helpers/CardFilterer';
// …
const filterer = new CardFilterer(cards);
```

**After:**
```javascript
// creates a separate chunk. no code runs until you click:
let filterer;
async function onOpenFilters() {
  const { default: CF } = await import(
    /* webpackChunkName: "card-filterer" */
    '../Helpers/CardFilterer'
  );
  filterer = new CF(cards);
  renderFilters(filterer.getFilters());
}
```

**Benefits:**
1. **Smaller initial bundle**
2. **Faster TTI** for pages that never open filters  
3. **Explicit loading** gives you a hook for showing a spinner or skeleton

